From: Alex <xnderv@yandex.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/nouveau: full reclocking for GeForce GTX 560 Ti (GF114, Gigabyte 1458:3515)

Board-specific, everything gated by nvkm_gf114_board() (nouveau.gf114_board=1 to try on
other GF114 boards, -1 to disable); on any other GPU the driver behaves like upstream.
Derived from an mmiotrace of the NVIDIA 390 driver on this board (2026-10-20) and
verified step by step on the lab driver (2026-10-19..22):

- fb/ramgf100.c: memory reclock by replaying the five PMU scripts the NVIDIA driver
  uploads (135<->324<->2004 MHz) through nouveau's memx, including the host-side writes
  around them; display pause relative to the current 0x611200; wait for the display
  fetch to stop before blocking VRAM access (as gt215 nouveau does).
- clk/gf100.c: never set bit 4 of the PLL control after lock (the NVIDIA driver does
  not; with it the 900 MHz core PLL hangs the GPU); hubk06 PLL reference through the
  source divider; reclocking enabled for the known board; PTHERM clock slowdown (FSRM)
  engaged around every GPC and hubk06 clock source switch and released after it, waiting
  for FSRM_STATUS to report it active (as the NVIDIA driver does, which waits ~5/8 us by
  timer). Without it a 07->0f switch under load wedges PGRAPH (GPC/ROP busy, no traps):
  7 of 7 test runs hung within 16 upshifts; with it 4 of 4 runs, 240 upshifts, clean.
- clk/base.c: NVIDIA order: going up, voltage and core clocks first, memory last;
  going down, memory first (clk->core_first).
- pmu/fuc/perf.fuc: PERF process accounts GPU load with the PMU busy counters set up
  exactly like NVIDIA 390 (asleep until the driver starts it); gf100.fuc3.h regenerated.
- pmu/gf100.c: once-a-second governor on that load (-> 0f at >= 50 %, 03 -> 07 at
  >= 15 %, down after 15 s / 30 s of low load, 5 s minimum dwell) with a thermal brake
  upstream nouveau lacks (>= 90 C: no 0f, >= 95 C: 03). nouveau.gf114_gov=0 disables it.
- pmu/gt215.c: a reply from the firmware is awaited with a 2 s timeout instead of forever.
- dispnv50/disp.c: AVI InfoFrame ITC=1 as the NVIDIA driver sends.

Voltages (0.950 / 1.075 V) and PCIe link speed (2.5 / 5 GT/s) come from the VBIOS tables
through upstream code and were verified to match the NVIDIA driver bit for bit.

